## Incorporating metal nanoparticles in porous materials via selective heating effect using microwave
Yingyu Zhou, Jiacheng Liu, Fuyuan Sun, Junchen Ouyang, Ruifa Su, Fanchen Meng, Yongqi Luo, Cheng Xu, Weina Zhang, Suoying Zhang (✉), and Fengwei Huo (✉)
Key Laboratory of Flexible Electronics (KLOFE) & Institute of Advanced Materials (IAM), Nanjing Tech University, Nanjing 211816, China
© Tsinghua University Press 2023  Received: 17 July 2023 / Revised: 12 August 2023 / Accepted: 13 August 2023
## ABSTRACT
Metal nanoparticle@porous material composites have attracted increasing attention due to their excellent synergistic catalytic performance. However, it is a challenge to introduce metal nanoparticles into cavities of porous materials without agglomeration on the exterior. Despite the progress achieved, a universal approach that can integrate different kinds of metal nanoparticles and porous materials is still highly desirable. Here we report a facile and general approach to fabricating metal nanoparticle@porous materials by microwave-triggered selective heating. The microwave can pass through the non-polar solvent and act on the polar solvent in the porous materials, causing the polar solvent to be heated, vaporized, and away from the pores of porous materials. The local void produced by the escape of polar solvent facilitates non-polar solvent containing metallic precursor to be dragged into the narrow pores, followed by further reduction, resulting in the complete encapsulation of nanoparticles. A series of metal nanoparticles@porous materials, ranging from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) to zeolites, are successfully prepared by this method and show excellent size selectivity in catalytic reactions.
## KEYWORDS
porous material, nanoparticle encapsulation, microwave synthesis, selective heating effect, selective catalysis
## 1 Introduction
Metal nanoparticles (MNPs) are receiving immense interest in catalysis owing to their excellent performances in hydrogenation reactions [1], dehydrogenation reactions [2], and oxidation reactions [3]. However, due to the high surface energy and thermodynamic instability of MNPs, they are tending to aggregate and grow during catalytic reactions, leading to a loss of activity and stability. Thus, control of the size and dispersion of MNPs has always been a major concern in achieving catalysts with high and stable activity. To this end, encapsulation of metal nanoparticles within porous materials offers inherent environments for spatial confinement to reduce the size of MNPs and prevent them from aggregation [4–6]. Besides, the encapsulated MNPs can also cooperate with porous materials to achieve efficient synergistic catalysis, such as site, shape, and site selective catalysis [7–10].At present, various synthetic strategies for introducing MNPs into porous materials have been developed. Generally, they can be divided into two categories: synthesis of porous materials prior to the formation of MNPs followed by the formation of MNPs in the porous materials (ship-in-a-bottle) and introduction of presynthesized MNPs during the growth of porous materials (bottle-around-ship) [11,12]. In the first strategy, methods including solution impregnation [13–17], the double-solvent approach [18–23], chemical vapor deposition [24–28], solid grinding [29, 30], and thermal decomposition [31, 32] have been adopted to obtain MNPs@porous materials. These methods provide surface-clean MNPs with exposed active sites for catalysis, but lack of
At present, various synthetic strategies for introducing MNPs into porous materials have been developed. Generally, they can be divided into two categories: synthesis of porous materials prior to the formation of MNPs followed by the formation of MNPs in the porous materials (ship-in-a-bottle) and introduction of presynthesized MNPs during the growth of porous materials (bottlearound-ship) [11,12]. In the first strategy, methods including solution impregnation [13–17], the double-solvent approach [18–23], chemical vapor deposition [24–28], solid grinding [29, 30], and thermal decomposition [31, 32] have been adopted to obtain MNPs@porous materials. These methods provide surfacclean MNPs with exposed active sites for catalysis, but lack of
control over the size, shape, and composition of the MNPs. In addition, these approaches inevitably lead to the deposition of partial MNPs on the external surface of the porous materials, which may decline the selectivity and stability of the catalysts. In the second strategy, the size, shape, and composition of MNPs in the composites can be well controlled. Unfortunately, the introduction of MNPs into the systems disturbs the formation of porous materials, and only a small number of porous materials are suitable for this method. Besides, the binder (e.g., polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP), oleyl amine, and dendrimers), which is applied to disperse MNPs and reduce the effect of crystal plane mismatch, is difficult to entirely clean up and unfavourable for the complete exposure of active sites [33–36]. Therefore, despite the progress achieved, it is highly desirable to develop a general and facile method for the fabrication of MNPss@porous materials with a clean surface and controlled size.Herein, we report the application of the microwave selective heating effect to incorporate MNPs into porous materials. During microwave treatment, the polar solvent inside the porous material can be selectively heated and vaporized away from the inside channels of the porous material, leaving the interior of the porous material with a state of negative pressure. Subsequently, the non-polar solvent outside the porous channels, which carries functional molecules, is inhaled into the porous material by the compensation of the negative pressure, thus introducing metallic precursor into the porous materials (Scheme 1). Through this method, a series of MNPs@porous materials were successfully prepared, including Pt@UiO-66, PtPd@UiO-66, Pd@MIL-
Herein, we report the application of the microwave selective heating effect to incorporate MNPs into porous materials. During microwave treatment, the polar solvent inside the porous material can be selectively heated and vaporized away from the inside channels of the porous material, leaving the interior of the porous material with a state of negative pressure. Subsequently, the nonpolar solvent outside the porous channels, which carries functional molecules, is inhaled into the porous material by the compensation of the negative pressure, thus introducing metallic precursor into the porous materials (Scheme 1). Through this method, a series of MNPs@porous materials were successfully prepared, including Pt@UiO-66, PtPd@UiO-66, Pd@MIL
\(101(\mathrm{Cr})\), and \(\mathrm{Pd@MCM-41}\). Benefiting from full-encapsulation, the resultant MNPs@porous materials exhibit good selectivity in the selective hydrogenation of olefin.
## 2 Results and discussion
The Pt content in UiO-66 can be adjustable by tuning the precursor concentration in the non-polar solvent (Fig. S2 and Table S1 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 1(a), the Pt nanoparticles were successfully encapsulated in UiO-66 with a content of \(0.43\,\mathrm{\wt.\%}\) (determined by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES)) and the average size of Pt nanoparticles is about \(1.58\,\mathrm{\nm}\), illustrating the confinement of UiO-66's pores in the growth of Pt nanoparticles. By contrast, when the precursor was adsorbed on the surface of UiO-66, the resultant Pt nanoparticles would have a wide size distribution and the average size is \(6.7\,\mathrm{\nm}\), as shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images and the corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping confirmed the presence and well distribution of MNPs in UiO-66 (Fig. 1(b)). To track the solvent exchange process during the microwave treatment, the methanol content in the nonpolar solution was monitored, as shown in Fig. 1(c). Compared with natural diffusion, the content of methanol in toluene increases significantly under the microwave treatment at the beginning. It indicated that microwaves can heat the methanol selectively and facilitate the escape of methanol from the porosity of UiO-66. Then, the escaped methanol is cooled down by the surrounding non-polar solvent (toluene), causing the rise of methanol content in toluene. With the prolongation of microwave
irradiation time, the methanol in the solution is continuously heated and evaporated, leading to the decrease of methanol content in toluene. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analyses showed that there is no obvious structural change in UiO-66 after the introduction of Pt NPs (Fig. 1(d)), indicating that the structural integrity of the composite is preserved. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms were collected to evaluate the changes in the porosity of UiO-66 (Fig. 1(e)). Compared with the original UiO-66, the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface area of the Pt@UiO-66 is slightly decreased and the pore volume decreased significantly. These can be attributed to the occupation of Pt particles in the pores or cavities of UiO-66, indicating the successful encapsulation of Pt (Fig. 1(f)). To verify the role of selective heating by microwave, the degassed UiO-66 (filled with air) was also conducted in the same condition. However, it is difficult to find the Pt nanoparticle in the product, illustrating that precursors can hardly diffuse into the pores of MOFs (Fig. S4 in the ESM). On the contrary, once the pores were filled with a polar solvent, methanol or water can infuse Pt into the pores of UiO-66 (Fig. S5 in the ESM).
Moreover, multiple metallic precursors can be simultaneously
introduced in porous materials to fabricate \(\mathrm{MNPs}@\) porous materials. As shown in TEM images and elemental mappings (Figs. 2(a)–2(d)), the hazy contrast of nanoparticles hints at the encapsulation of nanoparticles in MOFs and the presence of bimetal indicated the successful fabrication of \(\mathrm{PtPd@UiO-66}\). Besides, this method is also applicable to other MOFs, and \(\mathrm{Pd@MIL-101(Cr)}\) can be easily achieved, as shown in Figs. 2(e)–2(h). Notably, the particle size of Pd in \(\mathrm{Pd@MIL-101(Cr)}\) is apparently larger than that in UiO-66, which can be ascribed to the larger pore size of MII-101. This phenomenon verified that the pores of porous material can not only limit the particle size of encapsulated metals but also regulate their particle size.
As another classical porous material, zeolites were applied to verify the universality of the method as well. TEM images and elemental mapping of \(\mathrm{Pd@MCM-41}\) confirmed that the nanoparticles are successfully encapsulated in MCM-41 (Figs. 3(a)–3(c)). In \(\mathrm{Pd@MCM-41}\), the size of metal nanoparticles is limited to about \(3.2\;\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the pore size of zeolites \((3.8\ \mathrm{nm})\). Subsequently, XRD further confirmed that the crystal structure of zeolite treated by microwave remains unchanged, as shown in Fig. 3(d), illustrating that the proposed method is mild and applicable to zeolite.
In order to further prove that selective heating by microwave
can promote the exchange of the external solution with the internal solvent, perylene, which is a fluorescent molecular was introduced to the UiO-66 with and without microwave irradiation, respectively (denoted as Mw-perylene@UiO-66 and Soak-perylene@UiO-66, respectively). From the fluorescence spectroscopic plots in Fig. 4(a), the photoluminescence (PL) intensity of Mw-perylene@UiO-66 was twice the intensity of Soak-perylene@UiO-66, indicating that more perylene molecules were incorporated into MOFs under the microwave. Afterward, the fluorescence quenching experiment of perylene was conducted. When adding a quenching additive with a small molecule, iodomethane \((\sim2.7\mathrm{~\AA~})\) can pass through the window of UiO-66 and quench the perylene in the UiO-66 (Fig. 4(b)). However, when quenched by 5-(bromomethyl) undecane \((\sim7.5\mathrm{~\AA~})\) that has a larger size than the window of UiO-66, the PL intensity of Mw-perylene@UiO-66 remains almost identical to that before the quenching (Fig. 4(c) and Fig. S6 in the ESM). The quenching test further confirmed that most guest molecules can be fully encapsulated into UiO-66 through the selective heating effect of microwave, which is a prerequisite to exploiting synergistic catalysis of nanoparticles and porous materials. Subsequently, the synergistic catalysis of \(\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66}\) was demonstrated by selective hydrogenation of n-hexene, cyclooctene, and tetraphenylethene. The window aperture of UiO-66 is \(6\,\mathrm{\AA}\), and the molecular sizes of l-hexene, cyclooctene, and tetraphenylethene are 3, 5.5, and \(6.7\,\mathrm{\AA}\), respectively (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The molecular size of 1-hexene is smaller than the window aperture of UiO-66. Therefore, 1-hexene can easily enter the UiO-66 through the pore and react with the Pt active center to produce n-hexane with a conversion of \(99\%\). The molecular size of cyclooctene is close to the window aperture of UiO-66. The diffusion of cyclooctene will be negatively affected by the UiO-66 channel to some extent, resulting in a decreased conversion of \(40\%\) (Fig. 4(d)). Correspondingly, since the molecular size of tetraphenylethene is larger than the window aperture of UiO-66, it cannot penetrate through the interior of UiO-66 to react with Pt, leading to zero conversion. Thus, the \(\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66}\) presents excellent size selectivity in olefin hydrogenation.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated a facile and general strategy for the preparation of \(\mathrm{MNPs}@\) porous material composites via the
selective heating effect of microwaves. A series of catalysts with core–shell structures including Pt@UiO-66, Pd@MIL-101(Cr), PtPd@UiO-66, and Pd@MCM-41 were successfully fabricated. As a demonstration, Pt@UiO-66 exhibited excellent size selectivity in olefin hydrogenation. The selective hydrogenation and fluorescence quenching experiment further proved the complete encapsulation of functional material rather than deposition on the external surface of porous materials by the proposed approach. More importantly, this strategy can be utilized to incorporate various types of functional materials with porous materials, providing a novel path for the design and development of composite materials.
